PRISM — third-party model and dataset notices

This notice identifies upstream resources used to create the selected PRISM
text-to-NDF models. The repository's Apache-2.0 license applies to PRISM code,
not automatically to third-party assets or trained model files.

1. E5-large-v2 text encoder
   Project: intfloat/e5-large-v2
   Source: https://huggingface.co/intfloat/e5-large-v2
   License: MIT
   Include the upstream MIT license and copyright notice with the encoder
   weights and tokenizer. PRISM's ONNX graph adds masked mean pooling and L2
   normalization around the pretrained encoder.

2. MERL BRDF Database
   Authors: Wojciech Matusik, Hanspeter Pfister, Matt Brand, Leonard McMillan
   Source: https://www.merl.com/research/downloads/BRDF/
   Dataset record: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.8101681
   License asserted for these training inputs: Creative Commons
   Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0)
   License: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
   Citation: W. Matusik, H. Pfister, M. Brand, and L. McMillan, “A Data-Driven
   Reflectance Model,” ACM Transactions on Graphics 22(3), 2003,
   https://doi.org/10.1145/882262.882343.
   Changes: MERL BRDF measurements were converted into normalized 64×64
   NDF-shaped proxies; the material dataset was used for VAE and text-model
   training. The proxies are not claims of recovered physical NDFs.

3. RGL Material Database
   Source: https://rgl.epfl.ch/materials
   Terms: CC0 unless a specific material is otherwise marked:
   https://rgl.epfl.ch/pages/lab/material-database
   Citation: J. Dupuy and W. Jakob, “An Adaptive Parameterization for
   Efficient Material Acquisition and Rendering,” ACM Transactions on
   Graphics (Proceedings of SIGGRAPH Asia), 2018.
   Changes: selected RGB/spectral BRDF records were converted to normalized
   64×64 NDF grids and used in model training/evaluation.

4. PRISM-trained model assets
   Assets: text prototype retriever and Residual V3 Zernike VAE decoder.
   License designated for release: CC BY-SA 4.0, subject to the terms above.
   Changes include BRDF-to-NDF proxy preprocessing, augmentation, model
   training, and ONNX export. Keep the assets separately identified from
   Apache-2.0 source code and MIT-licensed E5 files. Do not add technical or
   contractual restrictions that conflict with CC BY-SA 4.0.

